# libcss: a var() declaration competes at its own cascade position (patch 329)
# Generated 2026-10-29
# Base: seleron/vitabrowser (patch stack through 328)
#
# A declaration whose value uses var() is queued in cascade_style() and
# replayed after the whole cascade (MacSurf fixes1268d), with its rule's
# specificity and origin restored. css__outranks_existing() breaks a
# specificity tie with ">=", which is right only while declarations
# arrive in cascade order. The replay arrives last, so it beat every
# later declaration of equal specificity. wordpress.org's header sets
# `padding: 0 0 var(--wp--custom--margin--vertical) 0` and zeroes
# padding-bottom in a later @media rule. The navigation list kept 30px of
# padding, the closed menu container grew to 95px and the bar's items
# sat 15px above the header.
#
#  - select.h: prop_state gains a 24-bit `seq`, the cascade position of
#    the declaration in force. It packs into the existing bitfield word,
#    so the struct stays 8 bytes. The state gains current_seq/next_seq,
#    and css_pending_var a seq.
#  - select.c cascade_style(): a rule takes positions from next_seq. The
#    declaration at bytecode word w gets base + 2w + 1. A deferred one
#    that had pos words before it in its rule gets base + 2*pos, which
#    falls between its source neighbours, so order inside one rule holds
#    too. The replay restores the queued seq.
#  - css__later_or_more_specific(): the specificity test of
#    css__outranks_existing(). Higher specificity wins; on a tie the
#    later position wins. Inline, every newcomer is later, so behaviour
#    without var() is unchanged. set_hint() records seq 0 (hints precede
#    every rule).
#  - custom_properties.h / stylesheet.c: css_deferred_decl spends two of
#    its pad bytes on `pos`, set when the carrier style merges into its
#    rule (words already in the rule; saturates at 0xffff).
#
# Gate: CSS-var-order (VARORDER329, 9 legs: a later rule, a later @media
# rule and a later shorthand beat a var() declaration; order inside one
# rule both ways; higher specificity, !important and a later var() rule
# still win). Without this patch it reads 4/9.
